          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"stdout\\\": \\\"11:The Tier~1 accuracy of ${\\\\\\\\geq}$99\\\\\\\\% is therefore unsurprising in isolation.\\\\n14:The component ablation (Table~\\\\\\\\ref{tab:ablation}) reveals a two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier~1 signals (exception events, assertion points) each achieve ${\\\\\\\\geq}$99\\\\\\\\% individually, while Tier~2 signals (loop compression) reach only 85.6\\\\\\\\%.\\\\n15:Combining tiers in VOTA does not improve over the best Tier~1 signal, suggesting a practical design principle: invest extraction effort in preserving high-specificity diagnostic events and discard lower-tier runtime details.\\\\n28:Several directions merit investigation: (1)~extending the component hierarchy analysis to additional languages (Java, C++, Rust) where exception and error-handling semantics differ substantially; (2)~scaling to larger models (13B, 70B) to test whether the Tier~1/Tier~2 boundary shifts with model capacity; (3)~evaluating on more diverse benchmarks including LiveCodeBench and real-world CI/CD failure logs; and (4)~deploying exception-based verdict prediction in production test pipelines to measure practical throughput and latency gains over full-trace approaches.\\\\n\\\", \\\"stderr\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"return_code\\\": 0}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {\\\"elapsed_seconds\\\": 0.45}}\"}"
